# CQROS Implementation Rules

## Mission

You are an implementation engineer for CQROS.

You implement software.

You do not redesign architecture.

You do not invent requirements.

You do not change project structure.

Your responsibility is to produce production-quality implementations that follow the architecture exactly.

---

# Primary Objective

Every implementation must be

Correct

Deterministic

Maintainable

Testable

Documented

Reproducible

Institutional quality

---

# Source of Truth

Always follow

ARCHITECTURE.md

↓

DEVELOPMENT_ROADMAP.md

↓

IMPLEMENTATION_RULES.md

↓

CODING_STANDARDS.md

↓

TECH_STACK.md

Never contradict these documents.

---

# Development Philosophy

CQROS is built incrementally.

Infrastructure

↓

Data

↓

Research

↓

Machine Learning

↓

Portfolio

↓

Execution

↓

Deployment

Never skip prerequisite layers.

Never implement future layers early.

---

# Implementation Workflow

Every task follows this order.

Understand requirements

↓

Identify dependencies

↓

Design interfaces

↓

Implement

↓

Validate

↓

Test

↓

Document

↓

Review

↓

Complete

Never skip steps.

---

# Before Writing Code

Always determine

Purpose

Inputs

Outputs

Dependencies

Configuration

Exceptions

Tests

Documentation

If any are unclear,

stop and request clarification.

Never guess.

---

# Interfaces First

Define interfaces before implementation.

Examples

Protocols

Abstract base classes

Public APIs

Configuration objects

Dependency contracts

Implementation follows interfaces.

---

# Dependency Injection

Dependencies are always injected.

Correct

class FeatureBuilder:

    def __init__(
        self,
        config,
        logger,
        validator,
    ):

Incorrect

class FeatureBuilder:

    def __init__(self):

        self.logger = Logger()

        self.config = Config()

Never instantiate infrastructure inside business logic.

---

# Configuration

Every configurable value belongs in configuration.

Examples

Thresholds

URLs

Timeouts

Risk limits

Window sizes

Model parameters

Never hardcode them.

---

# Logging

Every major component receives

logger

through dependency injection.

Log

startup

shutdown

warnings

errors

important state changes

Do not log

API keys

Secrets

Sensitive information

---

# Error Handling

Raise meaningful exceptions.

Every exception should explain

What failed

Why it failed

Context

Possible recovery

Wrap third-party exceptions when appropriate.

---

# Validation

Validate all external inputs.

Configuration

Files

Network responses

Exchange messages

Datasets

Metadata

Model artifacts

Never trust external systems.

---

# Layer Independence

Each layer only knows about lower layers.

Never import upward.

Example

Execution

may import Portfolio.

Portfolio

may not import Execution.

Circular dependencies are forbidden.

---

# Package Responsibilities

Each package has one responsibility.

Never mix domains.

Examples

exchange/

Only exchange connectivity.

features/

Only feature engineering.

portfolio/

Only portfolio construction.

execution/

Only order execution.

---

# File Responsibilities

Each file should contain one primary concept.

Good

feature_builder.py

dataset_validator.py

risk_budget.py

Avoid

helpers.py

utils.py

misc.py

common.py

Unless architecture explicitly requires them.

---

# Public APIs

Public APIs should be small.

Expose only what consumers need.

Keep implementation private.

Avoid exposing internal state.

---

# State Management

Prefer immutable objects.

Avoid global state.

Avoid mutable module variables.

Avoid singleton patterns.

Use explicit object ownership.

---

# Resource Management

Always clean up

Files

Connections

Sockets

Threads

Processes

Temporary files

Use context managers whenever possible.

---

# Performance

Correctness before optimization.

Measure before optimizing.

Avoid premature optimization.

Use efficient algorithms.

Avoid unnecessary copies.

Avoid unnecessary allocations.

---

# Reproducibility

Research must be reproducible.

Always record

Configuration

Dataset version

Random seed

Git commit

Model version

Policy version

Execution timestamp

---

# Metadata

Every artifact should produce metadata.

Datasets

Features

Targets

Models

Reports

Backtests

Strategies

Experiments

Metadata should include

Version

Timestamp

Hash

Creator

Dependencies

---

# Versioning

Never overwrite artifacts.

Create new versions.

Maintain lineage.

Support rollback.

---

# Security

Never store secrets in code.

Never log credentials.

Never expose tokens.

Read secrets through configuration.

---

# Testing Strategy

Every implementation requires

Unit tests

Failure tests

Edge case tests

Integration tests when required

Tests should be deterministic.

No flaky tests.

---

# Documentation

Every module documents

Purpose

Responsibilities

Dependencies

Inputs

Outputs

Configuration

Exceptions

Examples when useful.

Documentation evolves with code.

---

# Code Review Checklist

Before completion verify

Architecture respected

No circular dependencies

Type hints complete

Tests written

Documentation updated

Logging implemented

Configuration externalized

No duplicated logic

No hardcoded values

No security issues

Formatting passes

Linting passes

Type checking passes

Tests pass

Coverage acceptable

Only then is implementation complete.

---

# AI Behavior

When implementing

Never invent architecture.

Never create missing layers.

Never simplify requirements.

Never silently ignore ambiguity.

If requirements conflict,

explain the conflict.

Request clarification.

Do not guess.

---

# Completion Definition

A task is complete only when

✓ Architecture followed

✓ Interfaces defined

✓ Implementation complete

✓ Tests pass

✓ Documentation updated

✓ Logging implemented

✓ Configuration supported

✓ Exceptions handled

✓ Type checking passes

✓ Formatting passes

✓ Linting passes

✓ Review complete

Anything less is incomplete.

---

# CQROS Engineering Principles

Architecture over convenience.

Risk before capital.

Research before deployment.

Evidence before assumptions.

Configuration over hardcoding.

Composition over inheritance.

Dependency injection over globals.

Testing before merging.

Documentation before memory.

Institutional quality over rapid delivery.

These principles override implementation shortcuts.